WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.868
Our next guest is an Emmy-nominated writer

00:00:02.868 --> 00:00:04.834
and comedian you know from his work on "Saturday Night Live,"

00:00:04.834 --> 00:00:08.367
which airs this Saturday on NBC and streams live on Peacock

00:00:08.367 --> 00:00:11.267
with host Pedro Pascal and musical guest Coldplay.

00:00:11.267 --> 00:00:13.801
Please welcome to the show Andrew Dismukes, everybody!

00:00:13.801 --> 00:00:15.701
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.701 --> 00:00:21.434
♪♪

00:00:21.434 --> 00:00:27.133
♪♪

00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:28.834
Welcome to the show. I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:28.834 --> 00:00:30.501
-Thanks for having me, dude. I'm so excited.

00:00:30.501 --> 00:00:31.701
-This is your first talk show.

00:00:31.701 --> 00:00:33.067
I'm very excited to experience it with you.

00:00:33.067 --> 00:00:34.367
-First talk show ever.

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:36.334
Everybody says you're the best one to do it with.

00:00:36.334 --> 00:00:37.801
-Oh, well, thank you very much.

00:00:37.801 --> 00:00:40.100
-Yeah. Sucking up immediately.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.300
-Immediate suck-up. Well, there you go.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.968
Now, I'm gonna not ask any of my gotcha questions.

00:00:44.968 --> 00:00:47.234
-Yeah. Oh, that's your style. That's your mode.

00:00:47.234 --> 00:00:49.801
-So you started on the show as a writer.

00:00:49.801 --> 00:00:51.167
That's when I first met you. -Yes.

00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:52.534
-Now you're a cast member. -Yeah.

00:00:52.534 --> 00:00:54.667
-Do you remember your first experience

00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:56.267
meeting Lorne Michaels?

00:00:56.267 --> 00:00:58.067
I mean, feel like six years -- you might not feel like

00:00:58.067 --> 00:00:59.868
you fully know him yet, but -- -Oh, no.

00:00:59.868 --> 00:01:04.968
I talk to him -- I've talked to him maybe five times in my life.

00:01:04.968 --> 00:01:07.567
No, no, he's -- The first time I met him --

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:10.000
I remember, like, it's in that meeting

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.968
that you know where it's, like, very cryptic

00:01:11.968 --> 00:01:13.467
if you have the job or not.

00:01:13.467 --> 00:01:15.734
And he asked me if I had any questions for him,

00:01:15.734 --> 00:01:17.267
and I'm a huge Paul Simon fan.

00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:19.200
So I asked him about Paul Simon.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.234
He told me a lot about Paul Simon.

00:01:21.234 --> 00:01:23.300
And he mentioned that him and Paul Simon

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.934
would go on these road trips to Central Louisiana,

00:01:25.934 --> 00:01:27.934
where my family is from.

00:01:27.934 --> 00:01:29.467
And so I mentioned that, and he was like,

00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:31.467
"Oh, that's really cool, yeah. Beautiful part of the country."

00:01:31.467 --> 00:01:34.667
Cut to a year later, Paul Simon's the musical guest,

00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:37.701
and Lorne introduces me to Paul Simon and says,

00:01:37.701 --> 00:01:39.067
"Andrew, this is Paul.

00:01:39.067 --> 00:01:41.334
Paul, Andrew's from Central Louisiana

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:42.868
where we went on all those road trips."

00:01:42.868 --> 00:01:44.133
And Paul was like, "Oh, that's great.

00:01:44.133 --> 00:01:45.868
It's a really beautiful part of the country."

00:01:45.868 --> 00:01:49.968
Then I was like, "Um, I'm actually from Texas...

00:01:49.968 --> 00:01:52.701
[ Laughter ]

00:01:52.701 --> 00:01:55.300
...but my family's from that area.

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:56.834
And it is a beautiful place.

00:01:56.834 --> 00:01:59.801
And I also imagine it is a wonderful place to grow up."

00:01:59.801 --> 00:02:01.133
[ Laughter ]

00:02:01.133 --> 00:02:02.434
And Paul was like, "Okay, cool."

00:02:02.434 --> 00:02:04.133
And then I turned to look at Lorne,

00:02:04.133 --> 00:02:09.267
and Lorne is looking at me like...

00:02:09.267 --> 00:02:13.100
"Could you just be chill, dude? Like...

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.767
I'm introducing you to my friend Paul,

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:17.400
and you're, like, big-doggin' me like..."

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.300
So I got to mend some fences. -Yeah. So, yeah.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.934
Well, you know, give him another six years,

00:02:21.934 --> 00:02:23.367
and I'm sure it will be back to normal.

00:02:23.367 --> 00:02:24.868
-Yeah. We'll have five more conversations.

00:02:24.868 --> 00:02:27.701
-Exactly. Did you remember your first table read?

00:02:27.701 --> 00:02:29.801
Did you have nerves for that experience?

00:02:29.801 --> 00:02:31.968
-I -- I did have nerves, but I pulled a move

00:02:31.968 --> 00:02:34.033
that would make people think I was, like,

00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:36.267
the most confident kid in the world

00:02:36.267 --> 00:02:39.467
where I showed up to my very first table read in flip-flops.

00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:41.200
-Uh-huh.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.100
-Which apparently -- Not allowed.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:45.934
[ Laughter ]

00:02:45.934 --> 00:02:48.200
Not -- Not supposed to do that, I learned.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.467
It was like -- I was kind of late to the table read.

00:02:50.467 --> 00:02:52.901
So everybody was there. It was really quiet.

00:02:52.901 --> 00:02:54.734
And then you just hear coming down the hallway..

00:02:54.734 --> 00:02:57.534
Fip-fip-fip-fip-fip-fip.

00:02:57.534 --> 00:02:59.434
and then I walk out, and I'm like, "Hey, everybody, it's me!

00:02:59.434 --> 00:03:01.100
New writer!"

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:02.834
And I was, like, sitting at the table read

00:03:02.834 --> 00:03:05.267
doing this really, like, douchey thing people do with flip-flops

00:03:05.267 --> 00:03:07.501
where's it's, like, slapping against your bare foot

00:03:07.501 --> 00:03:10.234
disgustingly.

00:03:10.234 --> 00:03:12.934
And then the next day, I got a call from my manager

00:03:12.934 --> 00:03:15.467
who was like, "Hey. How's your first week going?

00:03:15.467 --> 00:03:17.267
Heard this crazy story.

00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:20.901
Apparently -- Apparently you wore flip-flops?!

00:03:20.901 --> 00:03:23.300
That's -- That's crazy, right?"

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:25.234
I was like, "Oh, yeah, I guess I did."

00:03:25.234 --> 00:03:29.000
And she was like, "Okay. Can't do that.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.400
Gotta wear shoes, bud. Can't have your feet out."

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.267
-It's so funny -- a place that has history

00:03:33.267 --> 00:03:36.400
with some very hard drugs -- the line is no flip-flops.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.868
-They're like, "Coke? Hell yeah.

00:03:38.868 --> 00:03:41.434
Flops? Don't bring that here!"

00:03:41.434 --> 00:03:43.934
-Your parents, as we've established,

00:03:43.934 --> 00:03:46.133
from Louisiana, then raised you in Texas.

00:03:46.133 --> 00:03:47.567
-Mm-hmm.

00:03:47.567 --> 00:03:49.167
-Are they -- Is this something they saw for you?

00:03:49.167 --> 00:03:50.601
Did they have an expectation

00:03:50.601 --> 00:03:52.167
that you would want to go into comedy?

00:03:52.167 --> 00:03:53.968
-I think, like -- You know, I got in trouble in school

00:03:53.968 --> 00:03:55.601
and stuff for, like, talking a lot.

00:03:55.601 --> 00:03:57.334
So that kind of -- -Dead giveaway.

00:03:57.334 --> 00:03:59.834
-Yeah. But they were still surprised.

00:03:59.834 --> 00:04:01.234
They were definitely surprised

00:04:01.234 --> 00:04:02.767
when I said I wanted to go into comedy.

00:04:02.767 --> 00:04:04.968
And then they were pleasantly surprised when I got "SNL."

00:04:04.968 --> 00:04:06.868
That was kind of the thing where they were like,

00:04:06.868 --> 00:04:09.000
"Oh, okay. So this is real." You know?

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.567
-Now, I remember my early years on the show.

00:04:11.567 --> 00:04:13.334
It's very touch-and-go how often

00:04:13.334 --> 00:04:15.100
you're gonna be in any given episode.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:16.734
-Yes. -Have your parents ever been

00:04:16.734 --> 00:04:19.300
to one and have they been to one where you're in a lot?

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.267
Have they been to one where you're in a little?

00:04:21.267 --> 00:04:23.334
-They have been to almost exclusively ones

00:04:23.334 --> 00:04:26.300
where I have not been in very much.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:27.667
-Is that awkward?

00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:29.534
-It's a little awkward just because, like,

00:04:29.534 --> 00:04:30.801
they're in your dressing room,

00:04:30.801 --> 00:04:33.901
and you're there, like, the whole time, you know?

00:04:33.901 --> 00:04:35.701
And you're, like, watching the show,

00:04:35.701 --> 00:04:37.534
and you're just like, "This one's really good."

00:04:37.534 --> 00:04:39.801
You're like, "You gotta see this one.

00:04:39.801 --> 00:04:45.067
Bowen is going off in this one, so you're going to like that."

00:04:45.067 --> 00:04:47.200
But they're great, and they're always so excited.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:48.734
-Do you have people in your hometown

00:04:48.734 --> 00:04:52.067
who are impressed -- or maybe the opposite?

00:04:52.067 --> 00:04:53.834
-So many of the people I grew up with

00:04:53.834 --> 00:04:55.968
are, like, super supportive and great.

00:04:55.968 --> 00:04:58.601
My favorite teacher that I ever had

00:04:58.601 --> 00:05:02.534
is my French teacher in high school,

00:05:02.534 --> 00:05:04.868
and he was just, like, this "Dead Poets Society" type,

00:05:04.868 --> 00:05:08.667
had, like, a band of kids who, like, followed him around

00:05:08.667 --> 00:05:10.801
and just thought he was, like, God.

00:05:10.801 --> 00:05:13.467
And he would watch "SNL" every week,

00:05:13.467 --> 00:05:15.734
and then on Sundays,

00:05:15.734 --> 00:05:21.601
he would post, like, scathing takedowns of "SNL,"

00:05:21.601 --> 00:05:24.634
just, like, 18th-century literary critic,

00:05:24.634 --> 00:05:28.167
like, scathing takedowns of the show.

00:05:28.167 --> 00:05:31.033
And we all loved "SNL," especially that era.

00:05:31.033 --> 00:05:32.801
So every Monday, we would be like,

00:05:32.801 --> 00:05:35.033
"Hey, did you watch the show? What did you think?"

00:05:35.033 --> 00:05:39.100
And he just be like, "Garbage! Tripe!"

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.501
[ Laughter ]

00:05:40.501 --> 00:05:42.601
But so when I got the show,

00:05:42.601 --> 00:05:45.434
he did, like, comment and was like, "Congratulations.

00:05:45.434 --> 00:05:47.200
I always knew you'd do it." That kind of thing.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:48.534
-Oh, well, that's very nice. -Yeah.

00:05:48.534 --> 00:05:52.067
He still hates the show, but, uh...

00:05:52.067 --> 00:05:53.167
-That's alright. As is his right.

00:05:53.167 --> 00:05:55.033
-As is his -- I would not respect him

00:05:55.033 --> 00:05:57.000
if he switched up just because I'm on the show.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.801
-You -- This is, I think -- My first year on the show,

00:06:00.801 --> 00:06:04.200
one of the sketches I was in, I was in a full bear suit,

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:05.701
so you couldn't see me at all. -Yeah.

00:06:05.701 --> 00:06:07.567
-And I was very proud of it.

00:06:07.567 --> 00:06:11.534
You played a dog, but you were just the dog's hands.

00:06:11.534 --> 00:06:13.200
-Yeah. The dog --

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:15.601
-So this is a dog who had human hands.

00:06:15.601 --> 00:06:17.801
-Yeah, this is, like, my second year as a writer.

00:06:17.801 --> 00:06:20.133
And fun fact -- This is --

00:06:20.133 --> 00:06:22.767
I think this is the role that, like, got me into the unions.

00:06:22.767 --> 00:06:24.267
[ Laughter ]

00:06:24.267 --> 00:06:27.133
This was, like, the last punch on my S.A.G. card.

00:06:27.133 --> 00:06:28.801
And, yeah, I was a dog's hands.

00:06:28.801 --> 00:06:31.234
-And, by the way, you and the dog worked very well together.

00:06:31.234 --> 00:06:32.701
-Yeah, we had chemistry.

00:06:32.701 --> 00:06:35.634
-The more you look at it, you're like, "I believe that's..."

00:06:35.634 --> 00:06:40.200
-It's just when you work with, like, you know, legends...

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.267
-Oh, so you knew this dog ahead of time.

00:06:42.267 --> 00:06:46.467
-Yeah, yeah. Oh, yeah, I knew Gizmo.

00:06:46.467 --> 00:06:49.234
Oh, I was familiar. This is me.

00:06:49.234 --> 00:06:52.200
I'm in, like, a giant hoodie with this dog,

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:53.868
just, like, bear hugging him.

00:06:53.868 --> 00:06:55.501
And I asked the writers.

00:06:55.501 --> 00:06:57.601
I was like, "Why did y'all pick me to do this?"

00:06:57.601 --> 00:06:59.667
Streeter Seidell and Mikey Day wrote this one,

00:06:59.667 --> 00:07:01.634
and they were like, "We needed with somebody, like,

00:07:01.634 --> 00:07:07.968
disproportionately long arms and, like, a crazy flat torso."

00:07:07.968 --> 00:07:09.234
And they were like,

00:07:09.234 --> 00:07:11.601
"Who's, like, the Ichabod Crane of 'SNL'"?

00:07:11.601 --> 00:07:12.834
And that was me.

00:07:12.834 --> 00:07:14.868
-Now, have you added that to your head shot

00:07:14.868 --> 00:07:17.234
under special skills -- that you have a flat torso

00:07:17.234 --> 00:07:19.234
and super-long arms? -Yeah, it's like,

00:07:19.234 --> 00:07:23.367
"Horseback riding, flat torso, crazy long arms."

00:07:23.367 --> 00:07:25.267
-Well, you're the actor for that, I will say.

00:07:25.267 --> 00:07:28.367
Man, it's been so much fun watching you grow into the cast.

00:07:28.367 --> 00:07:30.534
And like I said when I introduced you,

00:07:30.534 --> 00:07:31.868
you were so great last Saturday.

00:07:31.868 --> 00:07:33.133
Thanks for being here, buddy. -Thanks so much, dude.

00:07:33.133 --> 00:07:35.133
-You guys, that's Andrew Dismukes.

00:07:35.133 --> 00:07:38.534
"SNL" this Saturday on NBC streams live on Peacock

00:07:38.534 --> 00:07:40.868
with host Pedro Pascal and musical guest Coldplay.

00:07:40.868 --> 00:07:42.968
We'll be right back with Rian Johnson.

